he Hollow Man was the house, wearing it like a coat.\n\nthe old caretaker Gus brought salt, iron, sage — the whole apotropaic grocery list. David Lindqvist brought a camera, a recorder, and the stubbornness that had gotten them this far. 'We document,' David Lindqvist said. 'Then we end it.'\n\nThe whispers became a voice. It knew David Lindqvist's name. It offered a deal: leave the town of Gray's End, leave a diary with blank pages that fill overnight, and walk away clean. David Lindqvist considered it for exactly as long as it took to be afraid — then refused."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Night', 'text': "the old caretaker Gus arrived — of course the old caretaker Gus did, called by the thing friends are — with headlights and a crowbar and no plan at all. Together they forced the Hollow Man back toward the nursery, toward a diary with blank pages that fill overnight, toward the broken place.\n\nDavid Lindqvist understood then what had to be done: not destroy a diary with blank pages that fill overnight, but complete it. Join the halves. Speak the name. Give the Hollow Man what it had wanted all along — to be seen, to be mourned, to be released.\n\nIt came on the anniversary, as David Lindqvist knew it would. The fog pressed against the windows of the town of Gray's End like a face. a diary with blank pages that fill overnight activated — played, filled, showed, rang, drew, unlocked — and the Hollow Man stepped out of the walls."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Aftermath', 'text': "a diary with blank pages that fill overnight, mended, sits on the mantel. It doesn't play, or fill, or show. It's just an object now — which is to say, it's just a story. David Lindqvist keeps it to remember: fear is a liar, face it.\n\nthe town of Gray's End is for sale again, and David Lindqvist is staying. Someone has to be here who knows. Someone has to keep the light on. That someone is David Lindqvist now.\n\nThe archives got a new entry, in David Lindqvist's handwriting: what happened, what worked, what it cost. Let the next tenant know. Let no one ever pretend again."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue: The Light Kept', 'text': "And the town of Gray's End stands in its fog, ordinary and beloved, a house like any other — except that once, it was haunted, and once, someone stayed. That makes all the difference.\n\nSometimes a diary with blank pages that fill overnight catches the light oddly, and David Lindqvist feels the old chill. Then David Lindqvist touches it, whole and quiet, and remembers the choice: to face it, to mend it, to stay.\n\nYears later, David Lindqvist still walks the town of Gray's End at night, checking the rooms the way you check on sleeping children. The nursery is a study now. The cold spot is just a draft. Mostly."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

