om, the same name. the smiling man wasn't haunting the house. the smiling man was the house, wearing it like a coat.\n\nThe nursery door, locked for decades, opened for Ben Marsh like it had been waiting. Inside: dust, a small bed, and a music box that plays itself's twin — the other half of whatever had been broken that terrible night."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Night', 'text': "What followed wasn't a fight; it was a siege of the soul. the smiling man wore the faces of everyone Ben Marsh loved, starting with the medium Madame Zara, begging to be let in, to be believed, to be obeyed. Ben Marsh held the line with the oldest weapon: names, spoken true.\n\nIt came on the anniversary, as Ben Marsh knew it would. The fog pressed against the windows of Hollow Hill Sanitarium like a face. a music box that plays itself activated — played, filled, showed, rang, drew, unlocked — and the smiling man stepped out of the walls.\n\nThe scream, when it came, was relief. The cold spot warmed. The whispers stopped mid-word. Ben Marsh and the medium Madame Zara stood in the sudden ordinary silence of Hollow Hill Sanitarium, holding a music box that plays itself, whole at last, and wept like children."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Aftermath', 'text': "a music box that plays itself, mended, sits on the mantel. It doesn't play, or fill, or show. It's just an object now — which is to say, it's just a story. Ben Marsh keeps it to remember: fear is a liar, face it.\n\nMadame Zara sent a note: 'You did what I couldn't. The house is clean.' Ben Marsh framed it. Not from pride — from proof. Proof that the dark can be answered.\n\nDawn in Hollow Hill Sanitarium was just dawn — fog burning off, birds being birds. Ben Marsh slept fourteen hours and woke to the medium Madame Zara making pancakes like nothing had happened, which was exactly the right thing."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue: The Light Kept', 'text': 'Sometimes a music box that plays itself catches the light oddly, and Ben Marsh feels the old chill. Then Ben Marsh touches it, whole and quiet, and remembers the choice: to face it, to mend it, to stay.\n\nAnd Hollow Hill Sanitarium stands in its fog, ordinary and beloved, a house like any other — except that once, it was haunted, and once, someone stayed. That makes all the difference.\n\nfear is a liar, face it — Ben Marsh wrote it on the first page of the archive, and on the last. Whoever comes next will read it. Whoever comes next will know.'}]}

